{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothIndexGap","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothIndexGap","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothTaylorBound","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Exp"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_index_rational_of_taylor_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_index_rational_of_taylor_witness","kind":"theorem","line":238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndexGap.lean#L238-L293","doc":"**The hypothesis bundle is satisfiable and the conclusion is not vacuous.**\n\nTwelve hypotheses, several of them inequalities between constructed quantities, is exactly the\nshape in which a theorem can be true because nothing satisfies it.  This discharges every one of\nthem at `m = 1`, `r = (1)`, `P = x`, `α = 0`, `p/q = 0/1`, `K = 8`, `μ = 1`, `ν = ½`, where\n`hfinal` comes down to `16·e^{−4} < 1`.\n\n⚠ What it does **not** show: `γ = β − α` is `0` here, so the Taylor machinery is not exercised.\nA witness with `γ ≠ 0` is not available at small `m`: the constants `(box)²·H·(2B)^{Σr}·2^{Σr}`\nonly fall below `∏ q_h^{−r_h}` when `m` is large and the approximation exceptionally good, which\nis Roth's inequality itself and hence step 4's business.  Recorded rather than papered over. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.le_index_rational_of_taylor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.le_index_rational_of_taylor","kind":"theorem","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndexGap.lean#L190-L236","doc":"**Step 2, closed modulo one numeric inequality.**  The index of `P` at the rational point is at\nleast `ν`, given that `P` has index at least `μ` at `α`, that `p_h/q_h` is within `G_h` of `α`, and\nthat the explicit size bound falls below `1/∏ q_h^{r_h}`.\n\nThe last hypothesis is a comparison between explicit quantities — no polynomial, no index, no\nexpansion — and discharging it is step 4's parameter bookkeeping, not this estimate's job. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.coeff_translate_cast","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.coeff_translate_cast","kind":"theorem","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndexGap.lean#L172-L188","doc":"The `ℤ → ℚ → ℝ` transfer for a translated coefficient at a rational point. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_translate_add_le_exp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_translate_add_le_exp","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndexGap.lean#L137-L164","doc":"**The smallness bound, unconditional.**  `abs_coeff_translate_add_le_index` with `S` supplied.\n`G_h ≤ 1` is not a hypothesis: it follows from `G_h ≤ exp(−T_h)` and `T_h ≥ K/r_h ≥ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.prod_pow_le_exp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.prod_pow_le_exp","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndexGap.lean#L93-L135","doc":"**`S = exp(−K(μ−ν))`.**  The conversion the whole step turns on: `r_h·T_h ≥ K` takes the\n`1/r_h`-weighted index gap to the `T_h`-weighted size gap.  `T_h` is `−log G_h`, so in the\napplication `T_h = (2+δ)·log q_h` and `K = (2+δ)·L`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_gap_le_sum_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_gap_le_sum_div","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndexGap.lean#L70-L91","doc":"**The index gap is a lower bound on the weighted degree excess.**  `idxWeight r n ≥ μ` and\n`idxWeight r i ≤ ν` give `∑_h (n_h ∸ i_h)/r_h ≥ μ − ν` — truncated subtraction included, because\ntruncation only raises the exponents. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.idxWeight_cast","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.idxWeight_cast","kind":"theorem","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndexGap.lean#L63-L68","doc":"`idxWeight`, cast to `ℝ` and summed over all coordinates. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sub_le_cast_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sub_le_cast_sub","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothIndexGap.lean#L54-L61","doc":"Truncated subtraction dominates real subtraction. ","signatureChunk":0}]}